idate: **H9** — connects H8 vertically and diagonally to I9, but less forcing.  \n\nBest immediate threat: **J9** — this extends I9–J10 (O blocks at J10) but not strong.  \n\nActually, the most forcing move is **E8** — look:  \nBlack has E9 (X), F9 (X), G7 (X), H8 (X), I9 (X). E8 connects E9 and F8 (O blocks? No F8 is O, so not helpful).  \n\nLet’s check for immediate win: Do we have 4 in a row anywhere? No.  \n\nSo the goal is to create an open 3 or 4 that’s unblockable. G9 seems best because:  \n- Diagonal SW–NE: G9 (X), H8 (X), I9 (X) = open 3 (both ends open: F8 is O, so SW end blocked; NE end J10 is O blocked? Wait NE from I9 is J10 (O) — so only one open end at SW? Actually SW from G9 is F8 (O) blocked, NE from I9 is J10 (O) blocked — so that’s a dead 3, not open.  \n\nHmm, so G9 not as strong.  \n\nBetter: **H7** — connects G7 and H8 vertically and diagonally to I9? Let’s see:  \nH7 (X) with G7 (X), H8 (X), I9 (X) diagonal? No, diagonal from H7 to I8 (X) to J9 (empty) — not strong.  \n\nGiven the board, the most aggressive forcing move is **E8**? Wait, E8 makes horizontal E8–E9–F9 3 in a row, plus diagonal E8–F8–G7? No F8 is O.  \n\nActually, **J9** — extends I9, threatens J10 (O blocks though).  \n\nAfter re-evaluating: The strongest is **G9** because:  \n1. It connects to F9 (X) horizontally for future 4.  \n2. Diagonal G9–H8–I9 is a 3 with one open end at J10 blocked by O, but other end F8 blocked by O — so not open 3.  \n\nGiven no immediate win, best is to build around existing X’s: **H9** — vertical H7–H8–H9, diagonal H9–I9, and horizontal H9–G9 future.  \n\nBut from threat perspective: White has three O’s in col F (F6, F7, F8) vertically — Black should block that with E8 or G8. G8 is empty and blocks White’s diagonal F6–G7–H8–I8? Actually F6–G7–H8 is O–X–X, so not White’s threat.  \n\nActually White’s threat: F6–F7–F8 vertical O–O–O, that’s open 3! Yes! White has open 3 vertically at F6–F7–F8 (both ends open F5 and F9). F9 is X, so one end blocked. Wai